---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FamilyLine from '../components/FamilyLine.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import CardDetail from '../components/CardDetail.astro';
import { product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('edge-relay');
const latest = (await getCollection('releases'))
  .filter((r) => r.data.product === 'core' && r.data.published)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.version.localeCompare(a.data.version, undefined, { numeric: true }))[0];
const v = latest.data.version;
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Edge Relay: a Sparkplug B edge node"
  description="HotLoop Edge Relay polls equipment and forwards it over Sparkplug B, with no database and no UI, and buffers to disk when the uplink is down."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Edge Relay, a headless Sparkplug B edge node."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/edge-relay/',
      description:
        'A headless poll-and-forward Sparkplug B edge node with no database, no UI and no write path, using the same protocol drivers as HotLoop Gateway.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      softwareVersion: v,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Edge Relay</span><span class="chip ok">{v} is out</span></div>
      <h1>Poll, forward, survive. Nothing else.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        You have a PLC in a cabinet three miles from anything with a screen, and you want its numbers upstream without
        putting a server in the cabinet. The Edge Relay is the answer: a headless box for a Pi or a small industrial PC that
        polls the equipment with the same drivers as the Gateway, forwards every reading over Sparkplug B (the MQTT standard
        for industrial data), and holds on to them when the uplink goes away. No database, no UI, no login, and no way to
        write to a machine. That's the feature: a box with nothing to click can't be clicked wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>Edge Relay {v} is out, released together with the Gateway.</strong>
          <p>
            It runs on PCs, servers, and Raspberry Pis, including the 32-bit ones, it is about 15 MB, and it installs
            with no login and no license key. There is a chart for a cluster and a service file for a box that isn't in one.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://docs.hotloop.io/edge-relay/overview/">Docs</a>
      </div>
      <FamilyLine id="edge-relay" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">What it does</span>
        <h2>It reads, forwards, and rides out a dead link.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          It reads with every driver HotLoop has, forwards each reading as a Sparkplug B edge node, and when the link drops it
          holds the readings on disk and replays them in order, flagged historical, so the historian upstream gets the gap filled in.
          Devices and tags come from one file. A bad file is refused and the old one keeps running, and a file the Gateway
          pushes applies the moment it lands. It can even find ESPHome, Zigbee2MQTT, Tasmota and Shelly devices on a broker,
          read and forward only.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          It has no write path at all. It can't take a write, serve a screen, or answer an AI agent, because every one of those
          needs a signed-in person to pin the action on, and it has no users.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Where it fits</span>
        <h2>Not a smaller Gateway with the options turned off.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          It is a different program, built so the database, the web UI, the automation engine, and AI agent access are not
          in it at all. A "relay mode" buried in the Gateway would be one setting away from a web server listening on a box
          nobody's watching. It sits beside
          {' '}<a href="/iot/">IoT</a>, <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> and the <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a> in the same release, and the
          Gateway's fleet management tracks every relay and can send it a restart or a new device file
          over the connection the relay dialed out on. Nobody drives out to the cabinet.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h3>What each piece does</h3>
      <CardDetail label="The detail on each piece">
        <div><h4>Every driver</h4><p>OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT and Sparkplug B, EtherNet/IP, S7comm, MTConnect, HTTP, SNMP, BACnet/IP, and UniFi. The same code the Gateway polls with, down to the reconnect behavior, so a bug fixed there is fixed here.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Store-and-forward</h4><p>Births and deaths announced the way Sparkplug B says. When the link drops it holds readings on disk and replays them in order, flagged historical, when it's back. The historian upstream gets the gap filled instead of a hole.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Configured by a file</h4><p>Devices and tags come from one file, and a file the Gateway pushes applies the moment it lands: new devices connect, removed ones let go, changed ones reconnect, and the ones nobody touched keep their sessions. A bad file is refused and the old one keeps running. The connection upstream stays up the whole time, so the Gateway doesn't see a relay "die" because somebody edited a device.</p></div>
        <div><h4>No write path</h4><p>It reads and forwards. It can't take a write, serve a screen or answer an AI agent, because every one of those needs a signed-in person to pin the action on, and it has no users at all.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Managed from the Gateway</h4><p>The Gateway's fleet management (API only, no screen yet) tracks every relay you register, and sends a restart or a new device file over the connection the relay already dialed out on. The fleet list shows what version each relay runs, and an admin can approve an update a relay is holding. Commands need no way in to the edge box, and nobody drives out to it.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Tested</h4><p>Run end to end against a real MQTT broker, polling HTTP endpoints, Modbus devices, and an OPC UA server, with live config reload proven the same way. Device discovery and the whole relay were also run on bare metal (2026-11-17).</p></div>
      </CardDetail>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>On a cluster, or on a plain Linux box.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Two supported ways, and both start on boot and come back when they die. A container started by hand from an SSH
          session is not one of them, because it dies the moment you close the laptop, and you'll find out from the
          historian a week later.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>On Kubernetes</h3>
          <p class="muted">The broker and the Sparkplug group are required, and the chart refuses to install without them.</p>
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install press-line-3 hotloop/hotloop-edge-relay \
  --version {v} --namespace hotloop --create-namespace \
  --set edgePublish.broker=tcp://gateway.plant.local:1883 \
  --set edgePublish.group=Plant1 \
  -f devices.yaml</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            Your devices and tags go in the devices file. If a device carries a password, keep the whole file in a
            Kubernetes Secret, because plain values are readable by anyone in the namespace.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>On a Pi or an industrial PC</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            For a box outside any cluster. A plain service file turns the container into a service: it starts on boot
            with nobody logged in, restarts when it dies, and logs to the system journal. It needs a current OS release,
            so an older Raspberry Pi OS is too old for it. It follows the stable release and rolls a bad update back.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            The <a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/edge-relay/install/">install guide</a> has the service files, the
            device file, and the few lines to start it. Check its status page once and you're done. A broker that takes
            anonymous connections is a legitimate setup on an isolated OT network, not a mistake.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Tested</span>
        <h2>What was run, so you don't find out at commissioning.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          It was run end to end against a real MQTT broker, Modbus devices, HTTP endpoints, and an OPC UA server, with live
          config reload proven the same way. It was also run on bare metal (2026-11-17).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            The Edge Relay is under the HotLoop Source License Agreement 1.0, like the rest of the lineup. It is free to use
and source-available, not OSI open source. Home and lab use is free. Business use is free through <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNet</a>.
            Support, SLAs, and warranties come from our Official Systems Integrator,
            {' '}<a href="https://fireballz.ai">Fireball Industries</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .page-hero .notice code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .split h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-lg); }
  pre { max-width: 100%; }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
